# Line endings are the repository's decision, not each machine's.
#
# Without this, Git honours core.autocrlf, which is `true` by default on
# Windows: files are checked out CRLF while Prettier is configured for LF. The
# result is that `pnpm format:check` fails on every file in the repo on Windows
# but passes in CI, and `pnpm format` rewrites all ~400 of them — which buries a
# real change in noise and turns a one-line edit into a 400-file diff.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` normalises to LF in the index and checks out LF on every
# platform, so both see what Prettier expects.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary: never normalised, never diffed as text.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.webp binary
*.pdf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.keystore binary
*.jks binary

# Generated and vendored: excluded from diffs and from language statistics, so
# a lockfile bump does not read as twenty thousand lines to review.
pnpm-lock.yaml linguist-generated=true -diff
packages/db/src/generated/** linguist-generated=true -diff

# cmd.exe needs CRLF to parse a batch file reliably, and these are already
# stored that way. `-text` leaves them byte-identical everywhere rather than
# round-tripping them through LF on every checkout.
*.bat -text
*.cmd -text
